/**
 * Every navigation graph vertex and connection for one {@link Building},
 * returned in a single call by {@link NavRouter#graphForBuilding}.
 *
 * Plain value type with no identity.
 *
 * ### Why a bulk snapshot exists
 *
 * The map view needs node positions to draw markers, and needs them for the
 * whole building because the user can switch floors freely. Fetching
 * {@link MapNode} and {@link NavEdge} per floor, per interaction, would mean a
 * round trip on every floor change and a visible stall while panning. One
 * snapshot on page load is a few hundred small records for a building the size
 * of Grainger Hall, which is cheap to serialize and lets every subsequent
 * interaction be instant and local.
 *
 * Note the deliberate tradeoff: this does not scale to a whole campus in one
 * call. It is scoped to a single building precisely so the payload stays
 * bounded. A campus-wide view would need per-building paging instead.
 */
type NavGraphSnapshot {

  /**
   * Id of the {@link Building} this snapshot describes.
   */
  buildingId: !string

  /**
   * Every {@link MapNode} across all floors of the building.
   *
   * Empty when the building has no graph authored yet, which the UI should
   * surface as an empty state rather than an error.
   */
  nodes: ![MapNode]

  /**
   * Every {@link NavEdge} whose endpoints lie in this building.
   */
  edges: ![NavEdge]

  /**
   * Count of {@link NavGraphSnapshot#nodes}, included so the UI can show graph
   * coverage without walking the array.
   */
  nodeCount: !int

  /**
   * Count of {@link NavGraphSnapshot#edges}.
   *
   * A healthy authored graph has noticeably more edges than nodes; an edge
   * count at or below the node count usually means corridor connections are
   * missing or were only authored in one direction.
   */
  edgeCount: !int
}
